
<h2>18.4 scipy.optimize: Minimise, Fit, Solve</h2>
<p><strong>Optimisation</strong> means "find the inputs that make some number as small as possible". Training a model, fitting a curve, planning a route and pricing a product are all optimisation. <code>scipy.optimize</code> offers one function per family of problems. Pick by asking: is it a smooth function of a few variables (<code>minimize</code>), a model to fit to points (<code>curve_fit</code>), an equation to solve (<code>root</code>, <code>brentq</code>), or a straight-line objective with straight-line limits (<code>linprog</code>)?</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>minimize</code></td><td>General minimiser; choose a <code>method</code></td><td><code>optimize.minimize(f, x0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>minimize(method="BFGS")</code></td><td>Smooth, unconstrained, uses gradients</td><td><code>minimize(f, x0, method="BFGS")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>minimize(method="L-BFGS-B")</code></td><td>Smooth with simple bounds, many variables</td><td><code>minimize(f, x0, bounds=[(0, 5)] * 3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>minimize(method="SLSQP")</code></td><td>Smooth with equality or inequality constraints</td><td><code>minimize(f, x0, constraints=[c])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>minimize(method="Nelder-Mead")</code></td><td>No gradients, noisy or non-smooth</td><td><code>minimize(f, x0, method="Nelder-Mead")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>minimize_scalar</code></td><td>One variable, bracket or bounds</td><td><code>optimize.minimize_scalar(g, bounds=(0, 4), method="bounded")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>curve_fit</code></td><td>Least-squares fit of a Python function to points</td><td><code>optimize.curve_fit(f, x, y, p0=[1, 1])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>least_squares</code></td><td>Robust or bounded nonlinear least squares</td><td><code>optimize.least_squares(resid, x0, loss="soft_l1")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>root</code></td><td>Solve a system f(x) = 0</td><td><code>optimize.root(F, [1, 1])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>root_scalar</code></td><td>Solve one equation in one variable</td><td><code>optimize.root_scalar(g, bracket=[0, 3])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>brentq</code></td><td>Fast bracketed root, needs a sign change</td><td><code>optimize.brentq(g, 0, 3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fsolve</code></td><td>Older system solver, still common</td><td><code>optimize.fsolve(F, [1, 1])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linprog</code></td><td>Linear objective with linear limits (HiGHS solver)</td><td><code>optimize.linprog(c, A_ub=A, b_ub=b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>milp</code></td><td>Linear problem where some variables are integers</td><td><code>optimize.milp(c, integrality=1, ...)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linear_sum_assignment</code></td><td>Best one-to-one matching from a cost matrix</td><td><code>optimize.linear_sum_assignment(cost)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nnls</code></td><td>Least squares with non-negative coefficients</td><td><code>optimize.nnls(A, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lsq_linear</code></td><td>Linear least squares with bounds</td><td><code>optimize.lsq_linear(A, b, bounds=(0, 1))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>differential_evolution</code></td><td>Global search for rugged functions, with bounds</td><td><code>optimize.differential_evolution(f, bounds)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>basinhopping</code></td><td>Global search by random restarts</td><td><code>optimize.basinhopping(f, x0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>dual_annealing</code></td><td>Global search by simulated annealing</td><td><code>optimize.dual_annealing(f, bounds)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Bounds</code>, <code>LinearConstraint</code></td><td>Objects that describe limits cleanly</td><td><code>optimize.Bounds(0, 1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>NonlinearConstraint</code></td><td>Constraint given by a function</td><td><code>NonlinearConstraint(g, 0, np.inf)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>approx_fprime</code></td><td>Numerical gradient check</td><td><code>optimize.approx_fprime(x, f, 1e-8)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>check_grad</code></td><td>Compare your gradient with a numerical one</td><td><code>optimize.check_grad(f, grad, x0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>rosen</code>, <code>rosen_der</code></td><td>A classic test function and its gradient</td><td><code>optimize.rosen([1, 1])</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: minimise a function of two variables</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.4.1</span> Minimise f(x, y) = (1 - x)<sup>2</sup> + 5(y - x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>, a curved valley whose bottom is at (1, 1). Start from (-1.2, 1).</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize

def f(v):
    x, y = v
    return (1 - x) ** 2 + 5 * (y - x ** 2) ** 2

res = optimize.minimize(f, x0=[-1.2, 1.0], method="BFGS")
print("success:", res.success)
print("x      :", res.x.round(4))
print("f(x)   :", float(res.fun) < 1e-8)
print("iterations:", res.nit, " function calls:", res.nfev)
print(res.message)
@@END
<ol><li><code>f</code> takes <em>one</em> array <code>v</code>, not two separate numbers. This is the contract for <code>minimize</code>.</li><li><code>x0</code> is the starting guess. Optimisers walk downhill from there, so a bad start can end in a poor place for non-convex functions.</li><li><code>method="BFGS"</code> estimates the slope numerically and builds a curved model of the valley as it goes.</li><li>Always check <code>res.success</code> and <code>res.message</code>; a run can stop early and still return numbers.</li><li>We print only whether <code>res.fun</code> is nearly zero, because the exact tiny value is noise; <code>res.nit</code> and <code>res.nfev</code> show the work done.</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_optimize.png" alt="Contour plot of a curved valley with BFGS steps zig-zagging to the minimum marked with a star" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The BFGS path from (-1.2, 1) to the minimum at (1, 1).</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> the closed curves are levels of equal f: tight lines mean steep ground. The orange path starts on the left, drops into the banana-shaped valley and follows it to the star. Short steps in the valley show the optimiser feeling for the curve.</p>
<h3>Worked example 2: constraints and bounds</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.4.2</span> Minimise x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> subject to x + y at least 1, and both between 0 and 2. By symmetry the answer is (0.5, 0.5).</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize

obj = lambda v: v[0] ** 2 + v[1] ** 2
cons = optimize.LinearConstraint([[1, 1]], lb=1, ub=np.inf)
bnds = optimize.Bounds([0, 0], [2, 2])
res = optimize.minimize(obj, x0=[2.0, 0.0], method="SLSQP", bounds=bnds, constraints=[cons])
print(res.x.round(4), round(res.fun, 4), res.success)
@@END
<ol><li><code>LinearConstraint([[1, 1]], lb=1, ub=np.inf)</code> reads: 1 times x plus 1 times y must lie between 1 and infinity.</li><li><code>Bounds</code> sets a box for each variable separately.</li><li>SLSQP handles both. The solution sits on the boundary x + y = 1, which is typical: constraints are "active" at the optimum.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 3: curve fitting with uncertainty (Recipe 3)</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.4.3</span> Support calls after a storm decay as a e<sup>-kt</sup> + c. Fit a, k, c to 30 noisy measurements and report 95% intervals.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize, stats

def model(t, a, k, c):
    return a * np.exp(-k * t) + c

rng = np.random.default_rng(5)
t = np.linspace(0, 10, 30)
y = model(t, 4.0, 0.5, 1.0) + rng.normal(0, 0.15, t.size)

popt, pcov = optimize.curve_fit(model, t, y, p0=[1, 1, 0])
perr = np.sqrt(np.diag(pcov))
dof = t.size - len(popt)
tcrit = stats.t.ppf(0.975, dof)
for name, v, e in zip("a k c".split(), popt, perr):
    print("%s = %.3f +/- %.3f   95%% CI (%.3f, %.3f)" % (name, v, e, v - tcrit * e, v + tcrit * e))
resid = y - model(t, *popt)
print("residual std: %.3f (true noise 0.15)" % resid.std(ddof=len(popt)))
@@END
<ol><li><code>model(t, a, k, c)</code> must take the x data first, then the parameters as separate arguments.</li><li>We generate data from known truth (a=4, k=0.5, c=1) plus noise so you can see whether the fit recovers it.</li><li><code>curve_fit(model, t, y, p0=...)</code> returns <code>popt</code> (best parameters) and <code>pcov</code> (their covariance matrix). Nonlinear fits need a sensible starting guess <code>p0</code>; the default of all ones can fail.</li><li>The square roots of the diagonal of <code>pcov</code> are the standard errors.</li><li>The 95% interval uses a t quantile with n minus the number of parameters degrees of freedom: n = 30 points, 3 parameters, so 27.</li><li>The residual standard deviation (dividing by degrees of freedom) should be near the true noise 0.15; if it is much larger, the model shape is wrong.</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_curvefit.png" alt="Scatter of decaying measurements with a fitted exponential curve and a narrow shaded uncertainty band" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The fitted curve with an approximate 95% band built from the parameter covariance.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> the orange dots are measurements, the blue line the best curve and the shaded band the range the curve could plausibly take. The band is narrow where data is dense and reliable. Points outside the band are fine: the band describes the <em>curve</em>, not individual measurements.</p>
<h3>Worked example 4: roots of equations</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.4.4</span> Solve x e<sup>x</sup> = 5 for x. Then find the point where a circle x<sup>2</sup> + y<sup>2</sup> = 4 meets the line y = x.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import optimize

g = lambda x: x * np.exp(x) - 5
x1 = optimize.brentq(g, 0, 3)
print("brentq x =", round(x1, 6), " check:", round(x1 * np.exp(x1), 6))

F = lambda v: [v[0] ** 2 + v[1] ** 2 - 4, v[1] - v[0]]
sol = optimize.root(F, [1.0, 1.0])
print("root:", sol.x.round(6), sol.success, " sqrt(2) =", round(np.sqrt(2), 6))
@@END
<ol><li>A root solver wants a function that equals zero at the answer, so we move the 5 to the left side.</li><li><code>brentq(g, 0, 3)</code> needs a bracket where <code>g</code> changes sign (g(0) is negative, g(3) positive). It is fast and cannot fail inside a valid bracket.</li><li>For several equations in several unknowns, write a function returning a list of residuals, then call <code>root</code> with a starting guess.</li><li>The circle meets the line where x = y = square root of 2, about 1.414214.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 5: a small linear optimisation (Recipe 4)</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.4.5</span> You have 40 driver-hours for zones A and B. A earns 12 per hour, B earns 9. Fuel limits: 3 units per hour in A and 1 per hour in B, with 90 units in total. Zone A can absorb at most 25 hours. Maximise profit.</p>
<table class="steps"><tr><th>Variables</th><td>a = hours in A, b = hours in B</td></tr><tr><th>Objective</th><td>maximise 12a + 9b, so minimise -12a - 9b (linprog only minimises)</td></tr><tr><th>Limits</th><td>a + b &lt;= 40; 3a + b &lt;= 90; 0 &lt;= a &lt;= 25; b &gt;= 0</td></tr></table>
@@PY
from scipy import optimize

c = [-12, -9]
A_ub = [[1, 1],
        [3, 1]]
b_ub = [40, 90]
res = optimize.linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=[(0, 25), (0, None)], method="highs")
print(res.status, res.message)
print("hours A, B:", res.x.round(2))
print("profit:", round(-res.fun, 2))
print("slack (unused) per limit:", res.slack.round(2))
@@END
<ol><li><code>c</code> holds the cost per unit of each variable. Negating turns maximise into minimise.</li><li><code>A_ub</code> and <code>b_ub</code> encode every "at most" row: each row of <code>A_ub</code> times the variables must be at most the matching entry of <code>b_ub</code>.</li><li><code>bounds</code> gives each variable a (low, high) pair; <code>None</code> means no limit.</li><li>Status 0 means an optimum was found. The solution is at a corner of the feasible region, which is where linear problems always have their answer.</li><li><code>res.slack</code> shows unused capacity: a slack of 0 means that limit is binding (fuel and hours are both used up).</li></ol>
<div class="callout red"><div class="lab">COMMON MISTAKE</div><div class="txt">Forgetting that <code>linprog</code> minimises, then reporting a negative "profit" or the wrong plan. Negate the objective going in, and negate <code>res.fun</code> coming out.</div></div>

<h2>18.5 scipy.integrate and scipy.interpolate</h2>
<p><strong>Integration</strong> adds up a quantity that changes continuously: area under a curve, total distance from a speed, probability from a density. <strong>Interpolation</strong> estimates values <em>between</em> known points. Both turn scattered or sampled numbers into continuous functions you can query anywhere.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>integrate.quad</code></td><td>Area under a function between two limits, with an error estimate</td><td><code>integrate.quad(np.sin, 0, np.pi)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.dblquad</code></td><td>Double integral over a region</td><td><code>integrate.dblquad(lambda y, x: x*y, 0, 1, 0, 1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.nquad</code></td><td>Integral in many dimensions</td><td><code>integrate.nquad(f, [[0, 1]] * 3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.quad_vec</code></td><td>Integrate a vector-valued function</td><td><code>integrate.quad_vec(lambda x: [x, x**2], 0, 1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.trapezoid</code></td><td>Area from sampled points (straight segments)</td><td><code>integrate.trapezoid(y, x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.simpson</code></td><td>Area from sampled points (parabolic pieces)</td><td><code>integrate.simpson(y, x=x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.cumulative_trapezoid</code></td><td>Running area, e.g. distance from speed</td><td><code>integrate.cumulative_trapezoid(v, t, initial=0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.solve_ivp</code></td><td>Solve a differential equation from a start value</td><td><code>integrate.solve_ivp(rhs, (0, 5), [1.0])</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>integrate.odeint</code></td><td>Older ODE solver, still common</td><td><code>integrate.odeint(rhs, y0, t)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.interp1d</code></td><td>Legacy 1-D interpolator (no new features)</td><td><code>interpolate.interp1d(x, y, kind="cubic")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>np.interp</code></td><td>Fast linear interpolation on sorted x</td><td><code>np.interp(2.5, x, y)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.CubicSpline</code></td><td>Smooth curve through every point</td><td><code>interpolate.CubicSpline(x, y)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.PchipInterpolator</code></td><td>Smooth but never overshoots the data</td><td><code>interpolate.PchipInterpolator(x, y)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator</code></td><td>Smooth, little wiggle near outliers</td><td><code>interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator(x, y)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.make_interp_spline</code></td><td>B-spline of chosen degree through points</td><td><code>interpolate.make_interp_spline(x, y, k=3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.make_smoothing_spline</code></td><td>Smooth curve that does not chase noise</td><td><code>make_smoothing_spline(x, y, lam=1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.UnivariateSpline</code></td><td>Smoothing spline with a smoothing factor <code>s</code></td><td><code>UnivariateSpline(x, y, s=1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.RegularGridInterpolator</code></td><td>Look up values on a regular grid</td><td><code>RegularGridInterpolator((xs, ys), Z)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.griddata</code></td><td>Interpolate scattered 2-D points to a grid</td><td><code>interpolate.griddata(pts, vals, (gx, gy))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>interpolate.RBFInterpolator</code></td><td>Smooth interpolation of scattered data in any dimension</td><td><code>RBFInterpolator(pts, vals)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spline.derivative(), .antiderivative(), .integrate(a, b)</code></td><td>Calculus on a fitted spline</td><td><code>cs.integrate(0, 6)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spline.roots()</code></td><td>Where the fitted curve crosses zero</td><td><code>cs.roots()</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: integrals of functions, checked against a known answer</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.5.1</span> Compute the area under the standard normal density from -1.96 to 1.96 with <code>quad</code>, and check it against the cdf. Then compute the average of a function over a range.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import integrate, stats

area, err = integrate.quad(stats.norm.pdf, -1.96, 1.96)
print("quad area = %.6f  (error estimate %.1e)" % (area, err))
print("cdf diff  = %.6f" % (stats.norm.cdf(1.96) - stats.norm.cdf(-1.96)))

val, _ = integrate.quad(lambda x: x ** 2, 0, 3)
print("integral of x^2 on [0,3]:", round(val, 6), " mean value:", round(val / 3, 6))

tail, _ = integrate.quad(lambda x: np.exp(-x), 0, np.inf)
print("integral of exp(-x) to infinity:", round(tail, 6))
@@END
<ol><li><code>quad(f, a, b)</code> returns two numbers: the value and an estimate of the absolute error. Unpack both and look at the error.</li><li>The first check matches the analytic answer from the cdf: a 95% central area of about 0.95.</li><li>The integral of x<sup>2</sup> from 0 to 3 is 3<sup>3</sup>/3 = 9, so the mean value over the range is 9/3 = 3.</li><li>Limits may be infinite (<code>np.inf</code>); the integral of e<sup>-x</sup> from 0 to infinity is exactly 1.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 2: integrate sampled data</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.5.2</span> A delivery van logs its speed (km/h) every 6 minutes for an hour. How far did it travel? Use the trapezoid and Simpson rules, and the running total.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import integrate

t = np.linspace(0, 1, 11)                      # hours
speed = np.array([0, 20, 35, 42, 45, 40, 38, 30, 22, 10, 0.0])
print("trapezoid:", round(integrate.trapezoid(speed, t), 3), "km")
print("simpson  :", round(integrate.simpson(speed, x=t), 3), "km")
dist = integrate.cumulative_trapezoid(speed, t, initial=0)
print("distance at each stop:", dist.round(1))
@@END
<ol><li>Eleven readings give ten 0.1-hour intervals. The arguments of <code>trapezoid</code> are <code>(y, x)</code>: the values first, the positions second. Swapping them is a classic bug.</li><li>The trapezoid rule joins neighbouring points with straight lines and adds the areas.</li><li>Simpson's rule fits parabolas through triples of points and is more accurate for smooth data; it takes <code>x</code> as a keyword.</li><li><code>cumulative_trapezoid</code> returns the distance travelled so far at each reading; <code>initial=0</code> keeps the array the same length as the input.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 3: a differential equation</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.5.3</span> App users grow logistically: dN/dt = r N (1 - N/K) with r = 0.8 per week and capacity K = 1000. Starting from 50 users, find N after 10 weeks and when it first passes 500.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import integrate

r, K = 0.8, 1000.0
rhs = lambda t, N: r * N * (1 - N / K)
sol = integrate.solve_ivp(rhs, (0, 10), [50.0], t_eval=np.linspace(0, 10, 101), rtol=1e-8)
print("N(10) = %.1f" % sol.y[0, -1])
exact = K / (1 + (K / 50 - 1) * np.exp(-r * 10))
print("exact = %.1f" % exact)
cross = sol.t[np.argmax(sol.y[0] > 500)]
print("passes 500 users at about week", cross)
@@END
<ol><li><code>solve_ivp</code> wants <code>rhs(t, y)</code> with time first, then the state. The state is an array even for one variable, hence <code>[50.0]</code>.</li><li><code>(0, 10)</code> is the time span; <code>t_eval</code> asks for output at 101 evenly spaced times; <code>rtol</code> tightens accuracy.</li><li><code>sol.y</code> has one row per variable and one column per time, so <code>sol.y[0, -1]</code> is the last value.</li><li>This equation has a known closed form, so we compare. Matching it to a few decimals is the verification habit you want.</li><li><code>np.argmax(condition)</code> returns the first index where the condition is True: the first grid time with more than 500 users.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 4: interpolation and filling gaps</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.5.4</span> Seven sensor readings are known. Estimate the value at x = 2.5 with three methods, then use a spline to get the slope there and the total area.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import interpolate

xk = np.array([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.0])
yk = np.array([0, 0.8, 0.9, 0.1, -0.8, -1.0, -0.3])

print("linear:", round(float(np.interp(2.5, xk, yk)), 4))
cs = interpolate.CubicSpline(xk, yk)
pc = interpolate.PchipInterpolator(xk, yk)
print("cubic :", round(float(cs(2.5)), 4))
print("pchip :", round(float(pc(2.5)), 4))
print("slope at 2.5:", round(float(cs.derivative()(2.5)), 4))
print("area 0..6   :", round(float(cs.integrate(0, 6)), 4))
print("zero crossings:", cs.roots(extrapolate=False).round(3))
@@END
<ol><li>Arrays <code>xk</code> and <code>yk</code> are the known points. Interpolators need <code>x</code> sorted in increasing order.</li><li><code>np.interp</code> joins neighbours with straight lines: at 2.5 that is the midpoint of 0.9 and 0.1, which is 0.5.</li><li>A cubic spline is a smooth curve through every point; PCHIP is smooth too but is built so it never swings above or below neighbouring data.</li><li>A fitted spline is an object you can differentiate, integrate and root-find. <code>extrapolate=False</code> keeps roots to the known range; the first root, 0, is simply the first data point (its value is 0).</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_interp.png" alt="Seven black points joined by a linear path, a cubic spline and a PCHIP curve that overlap closely" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Linear, cubic spline and PCHIP interpolation of the same seven points.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> every curve passes through all black points, so they only disagree between points. The orange linear path has corners. The blue spline is smoothest but may overshoot slightly near sharp turns; the dashed PCHIP stays within the neighbouring values. Choose PCHIP for data that must not overshoot (prices, counts).</p>
<div class="callout red"><div class="lab">COMMON MISTAKE</div><div class="txt">Using interpolation to <em>predict outside the data</em>. Splines are only trustworthy between the first and last x. A cubic extrapolated past the end can swing wildly. Use <code>extrapolate=False</code> or a real model.</div></div>

<h2>18.6 scipy.linalg and scipy.sparse</h2>
<p><strong>Linear algebra</strong> solves systems like A x = b, finds the best-fitting line (least squares) and splits a matrix into simpler parts (SVD, eigenvalues). <strong>Sparse matrices</strong> store only the non-zero entries, so a matrix of 10 million cells that is 99.99% zero takes a few kilobytes. <code>numpy.linalg</code> covers the basics; <code>scipy.linalg</code> is a superset with more decompositions and options, and always uses optimised LAPACK.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>linalg.solve</code></td><td>Solve A x = b (preferred over inv)</td><td><code>linalg.solve(A, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.inv</code>, <code>pinv</code></td><td>Inverse and pseudo-inverse</td><td><code>linalg.pinv(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.det</code></td><td>Determinant, zero means singular</td><td><code>linalg.det(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.lstsq</code></td><td>Best-fit solution of an over-determined system</td><td><code>linalg.lstsq(X, y)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.eig</code>, <code>eigh</code></td><td>Eigenvalues; <code>eigh</code> for symmetric matrices (faster, real)</td><td><code>linalg.eigh(C)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.svd</code></td><td>Singular value decomposition</td><td><code>U, s, Vt = linalg.svd(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.qr</code></td><td>QR decomposition</td><td><code>Q, R = linalg.qr(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.lu_factor / lu_solve</code></td><td>Factor once, solve many right-hand sides</td><td><code>lu = linalg.lu_factor(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.cholesky</code></td><td>Square-root of a positive-definite matrix</td><td><code>linalg.cholesky(C, lower=True)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.solve_triangular</code></td><td>Fast solve for triangular systems</td><td><code>linalg.solve_triangular(L, b, lower=True)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.norm</code></td><td>Vector or matrix size</td><td><code>linalg.norm(A, "fro")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.expm</code></td><td>Matrix exponential</td><td><code>linalg.expm(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.null_space</code>, <code>orth</code></td><td>Basis of the null space or column space</td><td><code>linalg.null_space(A)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>linalg.block_diag</code></td><td>Build a block-diagonal matrix</td><td><code>linalg.block_diag(A, B)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.csr_array</code></td><td>Compressed sparse rows: fast row slices and products</td><td><code>sparse.csr_array(dense)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.csc_array</code></td><td>Compressed sparse columns: fast column slices and solves</td><td><code>sparse.csc_array(dense)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.coo_array</code></td><td>Triplets (row, col, value): easiest to build</td><td><code>sparse.coo_array((v, (r, c)), shape=s)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.lil_array</code>, <code>dok_array</code></td><td>Cheap to change element by element</td><td><code>sparse.lil_array((3, 3))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.eye_array</code>, <code>diags_array</code></td><td>Identity and banded matrices</td><td><code>sparse.eye_array(5)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.random_array</code></td><td>Random sparse test matrix</td><td><code>sparse.random_array((100, 100), density=0.01)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.linalg.spsolve</code></td><td>Solve a sparse system</td><td><code>spsolve(A.tocsc(), b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.linalg.cg</code></td><td>Iterative solver for symmetric positive-definite systems</td><td><code>cg(A, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.linalg.eigsh</code>, <code>svds</code></td><td>A few eigenvalues or singular values of a big sparse matrix</td><td><code>svds(A, k=5)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.csgraph.connected_components</code></td><td>Group nodes of a graph</td><td><code>connected_components(G)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.csgraph.dijkstra</code></td><td>Shortest paths on a weighted graph</td><td><code>dijkstra(G, indices=0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sparse.hstack / vstack</code></td><td>Join sparse matrices</td><td><code>sparse.hstack([A, B])</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: solving a system, and why you never invert</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.6.1</span> Three products use three ingredients. Find the batch sizes x that use exactly 100, 120 and 80 units of the ingredients. Then see what happens with a nearly singular matrix.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import linalg

A = np.array([[2.0, 1.0, 1.0],
              [1.0, 3.0, 2.0],
              [1.0, 0.0, 4.0]])
b = np.array([100.0, 120.0, 80.0])
x = linalg.solve(A, b)
print("x       :", x.round(4))
print("A @ x   :", (A @ x).round(6))
print("det, cond:", round(linalg.det(A), 3), round(np.linalg.cond(A), 2))

B = np.array([[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0000001]])
print("cond of nearly singular B: %.2e" % np.linalg.cond(B))
@@END
<ol><li>Each row of <code>A</code> is one ingredient; each column is one product.</li><li><code>linalg.solve</code> factors the matrix (LU) and back-substitutes. It is faster and more accurate than <code>inv(A) @ b</code>.</li><li>Check by multiplying back: <code>A @ x</code> must reproduce <code>b</code>. This is the cheapest verification there is.</li><li>The condition number tells you how much errors are amplified. A value near 1 is healthy. Matrix <code>B</code> has rows that are almost multiples of each other, so its condition number is enormous (around 10<sup>8</sup>): small noise in the data would swing the answer wildly.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 2: least squares and the SVD</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.6.2</span> Fit price = w0 + w1 * size + w2 * rooms to 200 simulated homes with <code>lstsq</code>, then use the SVD to see how many directions the 5 columns of a redundant feature matrix really carry.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import linalg

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
size = rng.uniform(40, 160, 200)
rooms = rng.integers(1, 6, 200)
price = 20 + 3.0 * size + 12 * rooms + rng.normal(0, 10, 200)
X = np.column_stack([np.ones(200), size, rooms])
w, res, rank, sv = linalg.lstsq(X, price)
print("weights:", w.round(2), " rank:", rank)

base = rng.normal(size=(200, 3))
M = np.column_stack([base, base[:, 0] + base[:, 1], 2 * base[:, 2]])   # 5 columns, 3 independent
s = linalg.svdvals(M)
print("singular values:", s.round(2))
print("numerical rank :", int((s > 1e-10 * s[0]).sum()))
@@END
<ol><li>The design matrix <code>X</code> has a column of ones for the intercept, then one column per feature.</li><li><code>lstsq(X, price)</code> minimises the sum of squared errors without forming the normal equations, which is numerically safer. It returns the weights, residuals, rank and singular values.</li><li>The slopes land close to the truth (3 and 12); the intercept is noisier (about 23 against a true 20) because the noise has standard deviation 10. More data tightens all three.</li><li>The matrix <code>M</code> has five columns but two are sums of others. <code>svdvals</code> shows only three clearly non-zero singular values; the other two collapse to about zero. That count is the numerical rank, and it explains why such a matrix breaks ordinary regression.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 3: sparse matrices save memory and time</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.6.3</span> A user-by-product matrix has 20,000 users and 5,000 products, and each user bought about 10 products. Compare dense and sparse storage, then multiply by a vector.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import sparse

rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
n_users, n_items, per_user = 20000, 5000, 10
rows = np.repeat(np.arange(n_users), per_user)
cols = rng.integers(0, n_items, n_users * per_user)
vals = np.ones(rows.size)
M = sparse.coo_array((vals, (rows, cols)), shape=(n_users, n_items)).tocsr()

dense_mb = n_users * n_items * 8 / 1e6
sparse_mb = (M.data.nbytes + M.indices.nbytes + M.indptr.nbytes) / 1e6
print("dense  needs %.0f MB" % dense_mb)
print("sparse needs %.1f MB" % sparse_mb)
print("stored non-zeros:", M.nnz, " density: %.4f" % (M.nnz / (n_users * n_items)))

popularity = M.sum(axis=0)                 # purchases per product
print("most popular product id:", int(np.argmax(popularity)), "with", int(popularity.max()))
user_scores = M @ rng.normal(size=n_items)
print("scores shape:", user_scores.shape)
@@END
<ol><li>The COO format stores triples (row, column, value), the easiest way to build a matrix from a list of events. Duplicate (row, col) pairs are summed when converted.</li><li><code>.tocsr()</code> converts to compressed sparse rows, the format for fast arithmetic and row slicing.</li><li>Dense float64 storage costs 8 bytes per cell: 20,000 times 5,000 times 8 is 800 MB. The sparse version stores three small arrays.</li><li><code>M.sum(axis=0)</code> and <code>M @ vector</code> run directly on the sparse structure without ever building the dense matrix.</li></ol>
<div class="callout green"><div class="lab">KEY IDEA</div><div class="txt">Build in COO or LIL, compute in CSR or CSC. Row-wise work (feature rows for a model) suits CSR; column-wise work and direct solvers suit CSC. Prefer the <code>*_array</code> classes in new code; the older <code>*_matrix</code> classes have different <code>*</code> semantics (matrix multiply) and exist for compatibility.</div></div>
<h3>Worked example 4: a big sparse system and a graph</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.6.4</span> Solve a 100,000-unknown tridiagonal system (a 1-D heat-flow style problem), then find connected groups and shortest paths in a small road network.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import sparse
from scipy.sparse import linalg as spla
from scipy.sparse import csgraph

n = 100_000
A = sparse.diags_array([-np.ones(n - 1), 2 * np.ones(n), -np.ones(n - 1)], offsets=[-1, 0, 1], format="csc")
b = np.ones(n)
x = spla.spsolve(A, b)
print("residual max:", float(np.abs(A @ x - b).max()) < 1e-6, " x[n//2] = %.0f" % x[n // 2])

# roads: 0-1 (4 km), 1-2 (3), 0-2 (9), 3-4 (2); nodes 3,4 are a separate island
rows, cols, km = [0, 1, 0, 3], [1, 2, 2, 4], [4, 3, 9, 2]
G = sparse.coo_array((km, (rows, cols)), shape=(5, 5)).tocsr()
n_comp, labels = csgraph.connected_components(G, directed=False)
print("components:", n_comp, labels)
print("distance from 0:", csgraph.dijkstra(G, directed=False, indices=0))
@@END
<ol><li><code>diags_array</code> builds a banded matrix from three diagonals. Only about 300,000 numbers are stored instead of 10<sup>10</sup>.</li><li><code>spsolve</code> wants CSC format for its direct solver. A dense solve of this size would not even fit in memory.</li><li>We verify by multiplying back and checking the residual.</li><li>A graph is just a sparse adjacency matrix with distances as values. <code>connected_components</code> finds the separate islands (two here).</li><li><code>dijkstra</code> returns the shortest distance from node 0 to every node: to node 2 it is 4 + 3 = 7 rather than the direct 9. Unreachable nodes show <code>inf</code>.</li></ol>

<h2>18.7 scipy.signal and scipy.fft</h2>
<p>A <strong>signal</strong> is any measurement taken over time: sales per day, a heartbeat, a machine's vibration. Two ideas cover most uses. <strong>Filtering</strong> removes unwanted parts (noise, slow drift). The <strong>Fourier transform</strong> re-expresses the signal as a sum of waves, so you can see <em>which frequencies</em> it contains. Frequencies need a <strong>sampling rate</strong> <code>fs</code> (samples per second, or per day for daily data).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>fft.rfft</code></td><td>Fourier transform of real data (non-negative frequencies)</td><td><code>fft.rfft(x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fft.rfftfreq</code></td><td>The frequency of each rfft output bin</td><td><code>fft.rfftfreq(len(x), 1/fs)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fft.fft</code>, <code>ifft</code></td><td>Complex transform and its inverse</td><td><code>fft.ifft(fft.fft(x))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fft.fftfreq</code></td><td>Frequencies for the full complex transform</td><td><code>fft.fftfreq(n, d)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fft.next_fast_len</code></td><td>A length that transforms quickly</td><td><code>fft.next_fast_len(1001)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>fft.dct</code></td><td>Cosine transform (compression)</td><td><code>fft.dct(x, norm="ortho")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.butter</code></td><td>Design a Butterworth filter</td><td><code>signal.butter(4, 5, fs=100, output="sos")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.sosfiltfilt</code></td><td>Apply a filter forward and back (no delay)</td><td><code>signal.sosfiltfilt(sos, x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.filtfilt</code>, <code>lfilter</code></td><td>Filter with b, a coefficients</td><td><code>signal.filtfilt(b, a, x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.savgol_filter</code></td><td>Smooth with local polynomials, keeps peak shape</td><td><code>signal.savgol_filter(x, 11, 3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.medfilt</code></td><td>Median filter, removes spikes</td><td><code>signal.medfilt(x, 5)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.find_peaks</code></td><td>Locate peaks by height, distance, prominence</td><td><code>signal.find_peaks(x, prominence=1)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.peak_prominences</code></td><td>How much each peak stands out</td><td><code>signal.peak_prominences(x, peaks)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.welch</code></td><td>Power spectrum with less noise than a raw FFT</td><td><code>signal.welch(x, fs)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.periodogram</code></td><td>Simple power spectrum</td><td><code>signal.periodogram(x, fs)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.spectrogram</code></td><td>Frequencies changing over time</td><td><code>signal.spectrogram(x, fs)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.convolve</code></td><td>Sliding weighted sum (moving averages)</td><td><code>signal.convolve(x, w, mode="same")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.correlate</code></td><td>Find a pattern or a lag between signals</td><td><code>signal.correlate(a, b, mode="full")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.detrend</code></td><td>Remove a straight-line trend</td><td><code>signal.detrend(x)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.resample</code></td><td>Change the number of samples</td><td><code>signal.resample(x, 200)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.hilbert</code></td><td>Envelope and phase</td><td><code>np.abs(signal.hilbert(x))</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>signal.windows.hann</code></td><td>Taper to reduce spectral leakage</td><td><code>signal.windows.hann(len(x))</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: which frequencies are in my data?</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.7.1</span> A sensor sampled at 200 Hz for 2 seconds hides a 5 Hz wave of amplitude 1 and a 30 Hz wave of amplitude 0.5 under noise. Find them.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import fft

rng = np.random.default_rng(2)
fs = 200
t = np.arange(0, 2, 1 / fs)
x = np.sin(2 * np.pi * 5 * t) + 0.5 * np.sin(2 * np.pi * 30 * t) + rng.normal(0, 0.3, t.size)

amp = np.abs(fft.rfft(x)) * 2 / t.size
freq = fft.rfftfreq(t.size, 1 / fs)
top = np.argsort(amp)[-2:][::-1]
for i in top:
    print("peak at %.1f Hz, amplitude %.2f" % (freq[i], amp[i]))
print("bin spacing:", freq[1], "Hz; highest frequency:", freq[-1], "Hz (Nyquist = fs/2)")
@@END
<ol><li>Two seconds at 200 samples per second gives 400 points; <code>t</code> is in seconds.</li><li><code>rfft</code> returns complex numbers for frequencies from 0 up to fs/2. Taking <code>np.abs</code> gives strength; multiplying by 2/n converts it to the amplitude of the original wave (valid away from 0 Hz).</li><li><code>rfftfreq(n, 1/fs)</code> labels each output bin. The bin spacing is fs/n = 0.5 Hz, so frequencies that fall exactly on the grid (5 and 30) appear cleanly.</li><li>We sort amplitudes and take the two largest: 5 Hz with amplitude about 1 and 30 Hz with about 0.5.</li><li>Nothing above 100 Hz (the Nyquist limit, half the sampling rate) can be seen; faster waves would masquerade as slower ones (aliasing).</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_fft.png" alt="Left: a noisy wave over two seconds. Right: its spectrum with two sharp peaks at 5 and 30 hertz" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The same signal in the time domain and in the frequency domain.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> on the left the pattern is invisible in the noise. On the right, each tall spike is one hidden wave: its position tells the frequency and its height the amplitude. The flat low carpet is the noise.</p>
<h3>Worked example 2: filter noise away</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.7.2</span> Remove everything above 10 Hz from the same signal with a Butterworth low-pass filter, and measure the improvement.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import signal

rng = np.random.default_rng(2)
fs = 200
t = np.arange(0, 2, 1 / fs)
clean = np.sin(2 * np.pi * 5 * t)
noisy = clean + rng.normal(0, 0.5, t.size)

sos = signal.butter(4, 10, btype="low", fs=fs, output="sos")
smooth = signal.sosfiltfilt(sos, noisy)
print("noise rms before: %.3f" % np.sqrt(np.mean((noisy - clean) ** 2)))
print("noise rms after : %.3f" % np.sqrt(np.mean((smooth - clean) ** 2)))
@@END
<ol><li>The "clean" signal is a 5 Hz sine; we add noise of standard deviation 0.5, covering all frequencies.</li><li><code>butter(4, 10, btype="low", fs=fs, output="sos")</code> designs a 4th-order filter that keeps frequencies below 10 Hz. Giving <code>fs</code> means the cutoff is in Hz, not a fraction of Nyquist. The <code>sos</code> output (second-order sections) is the numerically stable form.</li><li><code>sosfiltfilt</code> runs the filter forward and then backward, which cancels the time delay (zero phase). A plain forward filter shifts the signal in time.</li><li>The root-mean-square error against the known clean signal drops by a large factor: most of the noise lived above 10 Hz.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 3: find the peaks (for example daily orders)</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.7.3</span> Daily orders have a weekly rhythm plus noise. Find the peak days and confirm that the period is 7 days.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy import signal, fft

rng = np.random.default_rng(8)
days = np.arange(140)
orders = 100 + 20 * np.sin(2 * np.pi * days / 7) + rng.normal(0, 4, days.size)

smooth = signal.savgol_filter(orders, window_length=5, polyorder=2)
peaks, props = signal.find_peaks(smooth, distance=5, prominence=15)
print("peak days:", peaks[:6], "... total", len(peaks))
print("gaps between peaks:", np.diff(peaks)[:8])

spec = np.abs(fft.rfft(orders - orders.mean()))
f = fft.rfftfreq(days.size, d=1.0)             # cycles per day
print("dominant period: %.1f days" % (1 / f[np.argmax(spec)]))
@@END
<ol><li>Orders are a baseline of 100, a 7-day wave of amplitude 20 and noise.</li><li><code>savgol_filter</code> fits a small polynomial in a sliding window of 5 days; it smooths the noise while keeping the peak heights fairly intact (a moving average would flatten them more).</li><li><code>find_peaks</code> returns indices and a dictionary of properties. <code>distance=5</code> forbids two peaks closer than 5 days; <code>prominence=15</code> requires each to stand 15 orders above its surroundings. These two arguments are what stop noise wiggles from counting.</li><li>The gaps between neighbouring peaks are mostly 7 days (6 or 8 where noise nudges a peak by a day).</li><li>The FFT agrees: with daily samples, <code>d=1.0</code>, frequencies are in cycles per day, and 1 divided by the strongest frequency is the period. We subtract the mean first so the zero-frequency bin does not dominate.</li></ol>

<h2>18.8 scipy.spatial and scipy.cluster</h2>
<p><strong>Spatial</strong> tools answer "who is near whom?". A <strong>KDTree</strong> splits space into boxes once so that each nearest-neighbour query skips most of the data. <strong>Distance functions</strong> measure similarity between rows. <strong>Clustering</strong> groups points that belong together; <code>scipy.cluster</code> offers k-means and hierarchical clustering (for the full machine-learning treatment see scikit-learn and the Machine Learning document).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does in plain English</th><th>Minimal example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>spatial.KDTree</code></td><td>Index for fast neighbour search in low dimensions</td><td><code>tree = spatial.KDTree(points)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tree.query(x, k)</code></td><td>The k nearest points and their distances</td><td><code>d, i = tree.query(q, k=3)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tree.query_ball_point(x, r)</code></td><td>All points within radius r</td><td><code>tree.query_ball_point(q, r=2.0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tree.query_pairs(r)</code></td><td>All pairs closer than r</td><td><code>tree.query_pairs(0.5)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tree.sparse_distance_matrix</code></td><td>Distances for near pairs only</td><td><code>tree.sparse_distance_matrix(tree, 1.0)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.cdist</code></td><td>Distance between every row of A and every row of B</td><td><code>distance.cdist(A, B)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.pdist</code></td><td>All pairwise distances inside one set (condensed)</td><td><code>distance.pdist(X)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.squareform</code></td><td>Convert condensed to square matrix and back</td><td><code>distance.squareform(d)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.euclidean</code></td><td>Straight-line distance between two points</td><td><code>distance.euclidean(a, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.cityblock</code></td><td>Manhattan (grid) distance</td><td><code>distance.cityblock(a, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.cosine</code></td><td>1 minus cosine similarity (direction only)</td><td><code>distance.cosine(a, b)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.mahalanobis</code></td><td>Distance that accounts for correlation</td><td><code>distance.mahalanobis(a, b, VI)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>distance.jaccard</code>, <code>hamming</code></td><td>Distance for yes/no vectors</td><td><code>distance.hamming(u, v)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spatial.ConvexHull</code></td><td>Outer boundary of a point cloud</td><td><code>spatial.ConvexHull(points).vertices</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spatial.Delaunay</code></td><td>Triangulation of points</td><td><code>spatial.Delaunay(points)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spatial.Voronoi</code></td><td>Region closest to each point</td><td><code>spatial.Voronoi(points)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>spatial.transform.Rotation</code></td><td>3-D rotations without gimbal trouble</td><td><code>Rotation.from_euler("z", 90, degrees=True)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.vq.kmeans2</code></td><td>k-means clustering, returns centres and labels</td><td><code>vq.kmeans2(X, 3, seed=1, minit="++")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.vq.whiten</code></td><td>Scale each column to unit spread first</td><td><code>vq.whiten(X)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.vq.vq</code></td><td>Assign points to the nearest centre</td><td><code>vq.vq(X, centres)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.hierarchy.linkage</code></td><td>Build the merge tree (Ward, average, ...)</td><td><code>hierarchy.linkage(X, "ward")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.hierarchy.fcluster</code></td><td>Cut the tree into flat clusters</td><td><code>hierarchy.fcluster(Z, 3, "maxclust")</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.hierarchy.dendrogram</code></td><td>Plot the merge tree</td><td><code>hierarchy.dendrogram(Z)</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>cluster.hierarchy.cophenet</code></td><td>How faithfully the tree keeps distances</td><td><code>hierarchy.cophenet(Z, d)</code></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Worked example 1: nearest neighbours the slow and the fast way</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.8.1</span> A courier app has 200,000 restaurant locations. For 2,000 customers, find the 3 nearest restaurants. Compare brute force with a KDTree.</p>
@@PY
import time
import numpy as np
from scipy import spatial

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
shops = rng.uniform(0, 100, (200_000, 2))
custs = rng.uniform(0, 100, (2_000, 2))

t0 = time.perf_counter()
tree = spatial.KDTree(shops)
dist, idx = tree.query(custs, k=3)
t_tree = time.perf_counter() - t0

t0 = time.perf_counter()
brute = np.array([np.argsort(((shops - c) ** 2).sum(axis=1))[:3] for c in custs[:200]])
t_brute = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 10          # scaled up from 200 to 2000 customers

print("same answer:", np.array_equal(brute, idx[:200]))
print("tree at least 10x faster:", t_brute > 10 * t_tree)
print("first customer, nearest shop distance: %.3f" % dist[0, 0])
@@END
<ol><li>Points are rows of a 2-D array: 200,000 shops and 2,000 customers.</li><li>Building the tree is a one-off cost; <code>query(custs, k=3)</code> then handles all customers in one vectorised call, returning distances and indices (each of shape 2000 by 3).</li><li>The brute-force version computes the distance to all 200,000 shops for each customer, then sorts. We time only 200 customers and multiply by 10 to save waiting; this is an estimate.</li><li>We first check that both methods agree, then compare speed. We print a comparison rather than raw seconds because timings vary between machines and runs; on this machine the tree was far faster.</li></ol>
<div class="callout cream"><div class="lab">REAL JOB</div><div class="txt">KDTrees shine in 2-D and 3-D (geo points, sensor positions) and degrade as dimensions grow past about 20, when nearly every point is almost equally far away. For embeddings use an approximate index or scikit-learn's neighbour search.</div></div>
<h3>Worked example 2: distance matrices and hierarchical clusters</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.8.2</span> Six customers have (annual spend in thousands, orders per month). Compute distances, then cluster them into 2 groups with Ward linkage.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy.spatial import distance
from scipy.cluster import hierarchy

X = np.array([[1.0, 2], [1.2, 2.5], [0.8, 1.8],
              [8.0, 9], [8.5, 9.4], [7.6, 8.8]])
D = distance.squareform(distance.pdist(X))
print("distance 0-1: %.3f   0-3: %.3f" % (D[0, 1], D[0, 3]))
Z = hierarchy.linkage(X, method="ward")
labels = hierarchy.fcluster(Z, t=2, criterion="maxclust")
print("labels:", labels)
print("merge heights:", Z[:, 2].round(2))
print("cosine distance of rows 0 and 3: %.4f" % distance.cosine(X[0], X[3]))
@@END
<ol><li>Each row of <code>X</code> is a customer; two groups are obvious by eye.</li><li><code>pdist</code> returns a flat list of the 15 pairwise distances; <code>squareform</code> turns it into a 6 by 6 table where <code>D[i, j]</code> is the distance between customers i and j.</li><li><code>linkage(X, "ward")</code> repeatedly merges the two closest groups. Each row of <code>Z</code> says which two were merged, at what height, and the new size.</li><li><code>fcluster(Z, t=2, criterion="maxclust")</code> cuts the tree so that exactly two clusters remain; labels start at 1.</li><li>Merge heights jump sharply at the last merge (joining the two big groups): a big jump is the signal that the previous cut was natural.</li><li>The cosine distance compares direction only, so two rows with similar proportions look close even if one is much larger. It is small here because both customers have a similar balance of spend and orders.</li></ol>
<h3>Worked example 3: k-means</h3>
<p class="problem"><span class="pl">Problem 18.8.3</span> Cluster 180 points drawn around three centres, then ask which cluster a new point belongs to.</p>
@@PY
import numpy as np
from scipy.cluster import vq

rng = np.random.default_rng(4)
pts = np.vstack([rng.normal(c, 0.6, (60, 2)) for c in ([0, 0], [4, 1], [2, 4.5])])
centres, labels = vq.kmeans2(pts, 3, seed=1, minit="++")
print("sizes:", np.bincount(labels))
print("centres:\n", centres[np.argsort(centres[:, 0])].round(2))
new_point = np.array([[3.8, 0.7]])
code, dist = vq.vq(new_point, centres)
print("new point joins centre", int(code[0]), "at distance %.2f" % dist[0])
@@END
<ol><li>Three groups of 60 points each, centred near (0, 0), (4, 1) and (2, 4.5).</li><li><code>kmeans2(pts, 3, seed=1, minit="++")</code> picks three starting centres smartly (k-means++), then alternates between assigning points to the nearest centre and moving centres to the average of their points.</li><li>Cluster numbering is arbitrary, so we sort centres by x before printing. They land near the true centres.</li><li><code>vq.vq</code> assigns new points to the nearest centre; it returns the centre index and the distance.</li></ol>
<figure><img src="images/lib18_cluster.png" alt="Three coloured point clouds with black X marks at their centres, a purple star query point and five red circles on its nearest neighbours" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>k-means centres, plus the five nearest neighbours of a query point found with a KDTree.</figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>How to read it:</strong> colours are the k-means groups and the X marks are centres. The star sits between clusters, so its five nearest neighbours (red circles) come from more than one group. Nearest-neighbour search and cluster membership answer different questions.</p>
